Managed files are CLI-owned: edit pluto.toml, then run `pluto generate`
(`--dry-run` previews) to rewrite them after manifest edits or a CLI upgrade.
It refuses hand-edited managed files and never overwrites authored files.

This is the explicit, unreleased candidate scoped-record profile. Read
pluto.toml
and README.md. Run pluto fmt, pluto openapi, pluto architecture check
--directory . --format json,
and pluto vet. Keep every file <=1,000 lines and preserve authored migrations.

All production Go is managed, including domain, operation workflow, narrow
ports,
SQL adapters, transport and composition. Do not edit generated source or add
hooks,
initializers, alternate handlers, raw SQL clients, reflection or unsafe escapes.
Extend the framework's supported contract when business rules need a new
pattern.
Use pluto add blueprint --file contracts.toml for atomic declaration changes.

Schema 10 owns independent operation authority and resource/domain/data
contracts.
Each protected use case shares one opaque identity/resource gate across HTTP,
worker and host-approved agent entry. Never create identity from actor/role
strings
or trust payload authority. Current verified sessions, tenant assignments, role
permissions and authoritative resource facts are refreshed before protected
work.
Roles are explicit catalog declarations; adding operations never assigns users.

Commands normalize once in the owning domain constructor. Private records,
rehydration and machine construction stay in their managed owners. State
decisions
are pure; evaluated changes bind to the exact
machine/scope/entity/source/version.
The data adapter atomically commits mutation, audit, typed outbox work and
required
idempotency completion. Pending or uncertain claims require reconciliation;
never
steal them or retry with a new logical key. Queries apply visibility before
LIMIT,
use stable scoped cursors and reject oversized exports. Cache display snapshots
only after fresh authorization; lifecycle guards use authoritative SQL facts.

Workers use configured credentials and typed bounded delivery, not payload actor
claims. Agent writes use host-issued opaque prepared intents and actual
execution
receipts. Model text never proves a write occurred. HTTP, worker and tool
adapters
must invoke the same sealed operation. Provider URLs are deployment
configuration;
external delivery remains at-least-once and requires stable-key deduplication.

Authored seams (candidate): declare, then write only authored role files.
- Business code lives in internal/rules/<group> (pure validation and
  invariants), internal/logic/<group> (authored operation bodies, declared
  `body = "authored"`) and internal/adapters/<group> (SQL behind declared
  `[[ports]]`; sqlc query sets via `[[queries]]`). Adapters use only the
  scope's `queriesOf(...)`; tenant and actor come from the verified scope.
- Signup that creates an org: declare `tenant_root = true` on the root
  `[[tables]]` entry and an operation with `scope = "provision"` (authored write,
  verified identity, no membership; generated code mints the tenant id). Pre-
  login lookup by slug uses `scope = "lookup"` with `[[lookups]]`.
- Same-transaction writes across groups: the port declares `tx_methods` and
  `tx_callers`; the caller operation lists the port.
- `[memberships]` binds roles to an app table; `active_column`/`active_values`
  deny suspended subjects. `[database] runtime_role` names the least-privilege
  role; a migration is proposed and startup refuses owner/BYPASSRLS roles.
{{- if .HasMachine}}
- Machine identity (gateways, agents): `[[principals]]` kind = "machine" binds a
  table; `scope = "enroll"` is the generated public enrollment (single-use
  hashed token, expiry, rate limit, binds the ed25519 key, tenant read from the
  token row). `auth = "machine:<principal>"` operations are verified by
  signature (`pluto-machine-v1`: method, path, query, body hash, timestamp,
  nonce) and run as a scope.Execution whose tenant is the machine's and subject
  its id. Machine permissions live on the principal, never on user roles.
  Register machines in an authored write with `tx.IssueEnrollment(ctx,
  principal, machineID)`; never accept a tenant or actor from machine input.
  Revoke by changing the state column; the next request is denied.
{{- end}}
- Declaration changes: edit pluto.toml (or pluto add blueprint), then run
  `pluto generate`. Full reference: the CANDIDATE.md in the Pluto CLI docs.

{{if .LocalCredentials}}
Local credentials{{if .OIDC}} and OIDC{{end}} are managed: sign-in creates a
live session through the runtime's single fenced manager, which also issues the
opaque operation identity. The only authored production file is
internal/auth/credentials/integrations.go (Sender, Ingress, Limiter, Policy).
Keep it to delivery and abuse controls; ARCH609 rejects imports of application
packages, Pluto authority modules, stores, database clients, reflect or unsafe.
Never log notifications, tokens or passwords.
{{end}}
Apply explicit database migrations before startup. No constructor creates
schemas,
users, role assignments or fixture credentials. Candidate dependencies are
explicit local replacements or verified exact pins in pluto.toml; do not invent
release versions, edit pins by hand or silently switch profiles.
Never log tokens, bodies, secrets, private records or provider payloads.
