# Working on {{.Name}}

Managed files are CLI-owned: edit pluto.toml, then run `pluto generate`
(`--dry-run` previews) to rewrite them after manifest edits or a CLI upgrade.
It refuses hand-edited managed files and never overwrites authored files.

This is the published compatibility profile. Protected operations are drafts:
canonical architecture checks and pluto vet report ARCH401 until explicit
migration
to supported identity/resource/data contracts. Compiling this profile does not
establish those guarantees. There is no compliance bypass flag.

Read README.md first. Run pluto fmt and pluto vet before submitting changes.
Keep every file <=1,000 lines; prefer expanded readable Go and 80 columns.

pluto.toml schema 10 owns endpoint/feature/capability metadata. *_gen.go is
CLI-owned.
Per-endpoint handlers and operation *_gate_gen.go are managed. Models, private
operation execute methods, composition and transport helpers are authored and
created once. Never add magic init registration. Add endpoints with the CLI.
Do not import HTTP/storage from operations or database adapters from handlers,
even indirectly. Define narrow interfaces in consumers; inject constructors.

HTTP primitives are app-local. Authorization uses the pinned published
pluto-rbac module. Authored access.Roles starts empty; adding endpoints must
never
grant roles automatically. Keep one compiled policy injected into route and
managed operation gates through Dependencies.Authorizer and
Dependencies.Memberships.
Every protected Execute refreshes exact actor/tenant membership; declared
permissions use published Authorizer.Authorize with current assignments. Caller
Principal.Roles never confers a grant. Do not call or export private execute,
embed/alias a sealed boundary, or add an alternate exported operation entry.
Unknown roles, nil authorizers and missing resolvers deny.
Use the published Pluto session manager and HTTP adapter; preserve exact Origin
checks for cookie writes and reject ambiguous credentials. Supply a trusted
credential provider;
verify membership before creating a tenant principal. Preserve verified tenant
scope in every domain-specific repository. Permissions do not prove resource
ownership; flags never grant authorization. Never log bodies, tokens or secrets.

Dependencies.Cache uses published Core cache primitives. Scope non-authoritative
data by application, environment and verified tenant/subject. Never cache
sessions,
credentials, roles or authorization decisions. Check identity and permissions on
every request, including cache hits. Use bounded TTLs and explicit invalidation.

OpenAPI derives from paths and concrete models. Regenerate with
`go run ./cmd/openapi`; pluto vet verifies freshness. Use native fuzz tests and
operation/HTTP behavior tests. PostgreSQL connectivity is optional; generated
operations explicitly return not implemented and do not persist data.

Per-endpoint `internal/app/<group>_<action>.go` bindings are authored
constructor
choices. Add consumer-owned ports to <Action>Dependencies and use the managed
New<Action>WithDependencies constructor from this binding when adding
repositories. Add typed fields to app.Dependencies and construct/inject them in
app.New. Generated group registries accept narrow Operations interfaces and
never
choose concrete implementations; additions preserve authored constructor wiring.

Apply migration 006_authentication.sql for account-backed evidence and
007_external_accounts.sql before the PostgreSQL v0.5 account adapter.
Local-password apps use password.Service with postgres.NewLocal for both
accounts
and sessions. Never use direct Manager.Start/session-only issuance for local
accounts. Keep delivery, shared account limits and trusted client/concurrency
Ingress explicit; missing integrations fail startup. Auth responses never enter
idempotency capture. Enrollment never grants membership or roles. No startup
DDL.

Jobs use the published Pluto Jobs runtime. EnqueueTx shares the exact business
transaction; never silently split enqueue and commit. Handlers validate tenant
ownership, honor cancellation and make effects idempotent. No auth challenge,
password, cookie or token belongs in durable job payloads. Preserve authored job
handlers and CLI-owned explicit registration. Run cmd/worker separately; no
startup DDL or implicit goroutines. Empty/unimplemented workers must not fake
successful delivery. Apply 005_jobs.sql explicitly to existing databases.

Declare use-case authority in independent operations. HTTP, jobs and tools must
reference one operation and use managed adapters. Portable job contracts belong
in internal/jobcontracts; no authored worker or agent business callbacks. Worker
construction and handler registration are managed; supply typed gate
dependencies
through app workerDependencies. Never call raw jobs.NewWorker or Bind callbacks.

{{end}}
